The commit above is the head of meta pull request #108, "Fixture v7: a non-2xx
answer is unavailable whatever its body says". That pull request is merged
with a merge commit, not squashed, so this commit lands on meta's main
unchanged; had it been squashed, the commit above would be replaced with the
squash commit, and the blob, bytes and sha256 would not change. Version 7 adds
five calling-service cases and five gateway cases and changes none: the center
answers 500, 401, 302, 503 or 404 with a valid active body, and the answer is
503 `centerUnavailable` on a calling service and the `background` lane at
the gateway, after one call. This package already decided on the status
before reading the body (src/center.ts, `if (!response.ok)`); the cases pin
it, and test/non-2xx.test.ts fails if that return is removed (clerk-client#13).

Version 6 (meta #107, commit 993fea9): only space, tab, CR and LF separate
scopes, and two top-level keys equal ignoring case are a duplicate; 2.0.1 is
the first release that passes it.

The sha256 is over the LF bytes of the blob as git stores it, which is what
`git show <commit>:fixtures/introspection.json` prints. test/conformance.test.ts
recomputes it on every run and fails if the vendored copy and this record ever
disagree, so a hand-edit of the copy is a red test rather than silent drift.

To re-vendor after meta changes the fixture:

  git -C ../meta show HEAD:fixtures/introspection.json > test/fixtures/introspection.json
  node -e "const c=require('crypto'),f=require('fs');const b=f.readFileSync('test/fixtures/introspection.json');console.log(b.length,c.createHash('sha256').update(b).digest('hex'))"

then update the commit, date, version, bytes and sha256 above. Change meta
first; a copy that leads the original is drift pointing the other way.

The conformance suite also asserts the sorted list of case names, so a case
added upstream fails here until it is implemented rather than being skipped.
